From: Grische <opensource@grische.xyz>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 22:28:32 +0100
Subject: wifi: ath10k: do not keep a freed HTT tx queue state pointer

Since commit 9ec34a86195a ("ath10k: fix Tx DMA alloc failure during
continuous wifi down/up"), ath10k allocates the HTT tx memory once and
reuses it for every later firmware start, even when that start boots a
different firmware. The tx memory includes the queue state buffer,
struct htt_q_state. ath10k_htt_tx_alloc_txq() and
ath10k_htt_tx_free_txq() allocate and free this buffer only if the
firmware that is running at that moment has the PEER_FLOW_CONTROL
feature. ath10k_htt_tx_free_txq() frees the buffer but leaves
tx_q_state.vaddr and tx_q_state.paddr pointing to it.

The UTF firmware that testmode boots can lack PEER_FLOW_CONTROL, for
example an API 1 utf.bin, which advertises no firmware features at all.
This allows the following sequence:

1. A start of the normal firmware fails after ath10k_htt_tx_start().
   The error path of ath10k_core_start() calls ath10k_htt_tx_free(),
   which frees the queue state buffer.

2. Testmode starts the UTF firmware. ath10k_htt_tx_start() allocates
   the tx memory again and sets tx_mem_allocated.
   ath10k_htt_tx_alloc_txq() returns before it allocates a queue state
   buffer, so vaddr and paddr still point to the freed buffer.

3. Every later start of the normal firmware reuses this tx memory.

Once the firmware switches to push-pull mode, the host writes the queue
state into the freed buffer. The next failed start of the normal
firmware frees the buffer a second time.

ath10k_htt_tx_free_txq() also skips the queue state buffer if the UTF
firmware is the running firmware when the tx memory is freed, so the
buffer and its DMA mapping leak.

Free the queue state buffer in ath10k_htt_tx_free_txq() whenever vaddr
is set, independent of the running firmware. Clear vaddr and paddr
after every free of the buffer.

With these changes, a start of the normal firmware after step 2 would
dereference a NULL vaddr in push-pull mode. Therefore allocate the
queue state buffer in ath10k_htt_tx_start() when it reuses tx memory
without one and the running firmware has PEER_FLOW_CONTROL.

Fixes: 9ec34a86195a ("ath10k: fix Tx DMA alloc failure during continuous wifi down/up")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5 sparse
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c | 19 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/htt_tx.c
@@ -415,14 +415,15 @@ static void ath10k_htt_tx_free_txq(struc
 	struct ath10k *ar = htt->ar;
 	size_t size;
 
-	if (!test_bit(ATH10K_FW_FEATURE_PEER_FLOW_CONTROL,
-		      ar->running_fw->fw_file.fw_features))
+	if (!htt->tx_q_state.vaddr)
 		return;
 
 	size = sizeof(*htt->tx_q_state.vaddr);
 
 	dma_unmap_single(ar->dev, htt->tx_q_state.paddr, size, DMA_TO_DEVICE);
 	kfree(htt->tx_q_state.vaddr);
+	htt->tx_q_state.vaddr = NULL;
+	htt->tx_q_state.paddr = 0;
 }
 
 static int ath10k_htt_tx_alloc_txq(struct ath10k_htt *htt)
@@ -450,6 +451,8 @@ static int ath10k_htt_tx_alloc_txq(struc
 	if (ret) {
 		ath10k_warn(ar, "failed to dma map tx_q_state: %d\n", ret);
 		kfree(htt->tx_q_state.vaddr);
+		htt->tx_q_state.vaddr = NULL;
+		htt->tx_q_state.paddr = 0;
 		return -EIO;
 	}
 
@@ -526,8 +529,18 @@ int ath10k_htt_tx_start(struct ath10k_ht
 	spin_lock_init(&htt->tx_lock);
 	idr_init(&htt->pending_tx);
 
-	if (htt->tx_mem_allocated)
+	if (htt->tx_mem_allocated) {
+		/* The tx memory can come from the start of a firmware that
+		 * did not need the queue state buffer.
+		 */
+		if (!htt->tx_q_state.vaddr) {
+			ret = ath10k_htt_tx_alloc_txq(htt);
+			if (ret)
+				goto free_idr_pending_tx;
+		}
+
 		return 0;
+	}
 
 	if (ar->bus_param.dev_type == ATH10K_DEV_TYPE_HL)
 		return 0;
